## 8. Deliverable 6 — workflow security review

### 8.1 Threat model and controls

| Threat | Control in the workflow | Residual risk |
|---|---|---|
| A non-owner triggers a production build | actor must equal `github.repository_owner` (`ZESCODE`); the repository has exactly one account with write access | a compromised owner account is gameable — inherent to any single-owner model |
| Release from a non-`main` branch | `github.ref_type == "branch"` **and** `github.ref == "refs/heads/main"` | none for the dispatch path |
| Release of an unreviewed/foreign commit | full-SHA format check + `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/main` + CI status must be `success` for that SHA | a green CI on a bad commit still releases — mitigated by the manual promotion step |
| Moving-branch race (SHA changes after dispatch) | the SHA is the unit of release; the checkout uses the SHA; ancestry is re-verified in Job B | none |
| Production secrets exposed to validation or PR workflows | Job A has **no** `environment:` and **no** `secrets.*`; the `production` Environment is attached only to Job B; `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions are minimal (`contents: read`, `actions: read` in A; `contents: read`, `deployments: write` in B); `persist-credentials: false` on both checkouts | none identified |
| Deployment to the wrong project/team | `.vercel/project.json` is written from the Environment secrets and then re-asserted against `GET /v9/projects/{id}` (`id`, `account.id`, `productionBranch`) — a mismatch aborts before any deploy | none identified |
| Token leakage in logs | `::add-mask::` on the token; no `echo $VERCEL_TOKEN`; no `set -x`; the pulled `.env.production.local` is deleted in an `if: always()` step; no artifact upload | a Vercel CLI error line could echo a header — the CLI redacts `Authorization`; verify on the first dry run |
| Accidental promotion inside CI | the file contains **no** promotion command (static scan: `vercel promote|rollback|rolling-release|alias|remove|redeploy` → none); the only `vercel` mutating call is `vercel deploy --prebuilt --prod` | none identified |
| Accidental alias movement by the workflow | after the deploy, the workflow asserts the current production deployment id is unchanged, `app.zes.codes` is still assigned to it, the staged deployment is not the current production deployment, and the staged deployment's alias list does not contain `app.zes.codes` | the generated alias `zes-builder.vercel.app` may still move (see 9.3) |
| Supply-chain risk of the build tooling | actions pinned to the majors already used in the repository (`actions/checkout@v7`, `actions/setup-node@v7`, `pnpm/action-setup@v4`); Vercel CLI pinned to `65.9.0`; `npm install --global vercel@65.9.0` (no `@latest`) | action tags are mutable majors — SHA-pinning is optional hardening, not enabled to stay consistent with the repository |
| DoS via repeated dispatches | `concurrency: {group: production-release, cancel-in-progress: false}` → at most one release at a time | a queued run can still be dispatched repeatedly by the owner |
| Secret exfiltration via a compromised dependency in the build | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` pins the dependency graph | the release build runs untrusted repo code on the runner — inherent |
| Workflow file itself tampered with | the workflow is committed and reviewed like any other code; `git.deploymentEnabled` blocks the *automatic* path even if a tampered workflow pushed | an attacker who can push to `main` can also edit the workflow — Barrier 2 still requires a Vercel Dashboard promotion |

### 8.2 Findings

- **S-01 — `requireAuth` is a no-op in `withSecurity`.** `app/lib/security.ts:315-452` accepts `requireAuth` in its options type but never evaluates it. Any future hardening that passes `requireAuth: true` would silently do nothing. (Relevant to C-8.)
- **S-02 — rate limiting depends on a trusted client IP.** `checkRateLimit` returns `unavailable` without a trusted IP (`app/lib/security.ts:96-121`, `app/lib/security.ts:358-366`) and the wrapper then answers **503**. On Vercel, `context.clientAddress` availability must be verified before wrapping routes with `withSecurity({rateLimit:true})`, otherwise the feature breaks in production.
- **S-03 — Environment secrets are not masked by default.** `VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET` is consumed in a shell variable; the workflow masks only `VERCEL_TOKEN`. The bypass value is never printed, but adding `::add-mask::` for it is a one-line hardening.
- **S-04 — the release workflow cannot self-test on a non-production project.** Job B is hard-bound to `prj_w2rAtbukOEw7tU35XYWW1KpZgQAa`; the "no promotion" behaviour is therefore validated by static analysis + the first real staged release, not by a throwaway project.

