## 14. C-8 — separate security review of the in-app Vercel deployment route (no behaviour changed)

**Files inspected:** `app/routes/api.vercel-deploy.ts` (490 lines), `app/routes/api.vercel-user.ts` (160 lines), `app/lib/security.ts` (477 lines), callers `app/components/deploy/VercelDeploy.client.tsx:183`, `app/components/chat/VercelDeploymentLink.client.tsx:107`, `app/lib/services/connectorActivation.ts:71`.

| Finding | Evidence | Assessment |
|---|---|---|
| The deploy route is **not** wrapped in `withSecurity` | `app/routes/api.vercel-deploy.ts:176` (loader) and `:243` (action) are exported raw; `api.vercel-user.ts:74` / `:157` *are* wrapped | no rate limit, no origin check, no method whitelist on the deploy route |
| The Vercel token is **caller-supplied** | loader: `app/routes/api.vercel-deploy.ts:179` (`?token=`), action: `:245` (body `token`) | the route is a proxy that acts with the caller's own credential — no privilege escalation *by itself* |
| The token travels in the **query string** | `app/routes/api.vercel-deploy.ts:179`; the app's own client does `fetch('/api/vercel-deploy?projectId=…&token=…')` (`app/components/chat/VercelDeploymentLink.client.tsx:107`) | the token lands in server access logs, browser history and `Referer` headers — a real, fixable leak |
| Deployments are created with **`target: 'production'`** | `app/routes/api.vercel-deploy.ts:384`, POST to `/v13/deployments` at `:419` | for the caller's own project; if a ZES-owned token is ever presented, it can deploy into ZES projects |
| No server-side Vercel credential is configured today | the project's 15 env vars contain no `VITE_VERCEL_ACCESS_TOKEN`; `api.vercel-user.ts:11-14` would fall back to `context.cloudflare.env` / `process.env` | the route **cannot** currently deploy ZES-owned projects unless the caller supplies a ZES token |
| `VITE_`-prefixed vars are **inlined into the client bundle** | `vite.config.ts:232-238` (`envPrefix` includes `VITE_`) | a future `VITE_VERCEL_ACCESS_TOKEN` in the Vercel project env would leak the token to the browser — never add one |
| `withSecurity`'s `requireAuth` is **not implemented** | `app/lib/security.ts:315-452` — the option is accepted but never checked | any hardening that sets `requireAuth: true` would silently do nothing (S-01) |
| Rate limiting depends on a trusted client IP | `app/lib/security.ts:96-121`, `:358-366` → 503 when unavailable | wrapping the route with `withSecurity({rateLimit:true})` may break it on Vercel unless a trusted IP source is configured (S-02) |
| Unbounded request payload | the action accepts an arbitrary `files` map (`:245`, `:358-378`) | resource-exhaustion / abuse surface on a public endpoint |

**Proposed remediation (separate phase, not implemented here):** (R-8a) move the token out of the query string — accept it only in the POST body or an `Authorization` header, and drop the GET loader's `token` parameter; (R-8b) wrap both handlers with `withSecurity` **after** confirming a trusted client IP on Vercel; (R-8c) implement `requireAuth` in `withSecurity` or introduce a real credential vault instead of the current no-op; (R-8d) allow-list ZES-owned project ids (e.g. `prj_w2rAtbukOEw7tU35XYWW1KpZgQAa`) and refuse deployments into them from a caller-supplied token; (R-8e) verify the token's account matches the target project's account before deploying; (R-8f) cap the number/size of uploaded files; (R-8g) never name a server-side Vercel credential with a `VITE_` prefix.

